  This section is a continuation of discussing errors, the importance of reporting them, the dangers of not, and how  machines aren't intelligent enough to stop us from making mistakes.

  Reporting Errors is the first part of this section. It's pretty self explanatory. We can hopefully catch errors in order to report them. It's like learning from your mistakes. And it helps to have a second opinion or pair of eyes to help catch mistakes too. This cycle of making and catching, and learning from mistakes will hopefully reduce the occurence of more errors, according to Norman.
  
  We're given three case studies, the first being the the Japanese philosphy of Jidoka, the Toyota Production system's principle for design. I'd never heard of this before, I actually almost thought it was an obscure car model. The Jidoka model translates to "automataion with human touch". This immediately makes me think of how AI projects and ideas are usually okay enough but still need human touch to really tie it all together for a finished product. This still takes human intelligence and skill. The book explains that on the production line, one of the crew is expected to cautch a teammember's mistake, even it means stopping the assembly line. This reminds me of when I worked at Cupcakin' a big bakery, practically a factory. We'd have hundreds of cupcakes to make each shift, almost a thousand sometimes with a shift of 3-6 people. We each held each other accountable for making sure each recipe, design, and their storage was handled properly. This was moreso done by the shift leader, but the crew was still expected to do this as well to have a speedy, nearly 100% accurate production. The amount of times I messed up and had to redo cupcakes was hilarious now, looking back at it. Sometimes even a whole tray with my piping facing the wrong direction and my shift leader would get so upset, reteach me, and make sure I knew how to frost correclty according to the standard. 
